  "info": {
    "description": "\nRestaurant reservation and food-ordering system — JC2001 BMIS Group 4.\n\n**Modules**\n\n| Module | Base path | Objective |\n|--------|-----------|-----------|\n| Health & metadata | `/health` | — |\n| Reservation (seat booking, holds, state machine) | `/api/v1/reservations` | (a) |\n| Ordering (menu browsing, order submission) | `/api/v1/orders` | (a) |\n| Staff backend (reservation list, status, orders) | `/api/v1/staff` | (b) |\n| Membership & loyalty (auth, points, tiers, coupons) | `/api/v1/membership` | (c) |\n| Ratings & reviews (order-verified, Bayesian ranking) | `/api/v1/reviews` | (c) |\n| Analytics (manager dashboard, recommender) | `/api/v1/analytics` | (e) |\n\n**Authentication** — JWT bearer tokens. Click *Authorize* and paste the token\nreturned by the login endpoint. Roles: `customer`, `waiter`, `manager`, `admin`.\n\n**Envelope** — every response, success or failure, has the shape\n\n    {\"code\": 0, \"message\": \"ok\", \"data\": {...},\n     \"request_id\": \"…\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-10-11T12:30:00Z\"}\n\n`code` is **0 on success** and a 5-digit business code on failure (`20001` slot\nalready booked, `30003` permission denied, `50001` internal error, …). It is not\nthe HTTP status: the status tells your HTTP client what happened at the transport\nlevel, while `code` tells your application *which* rule was violated. Switch on\n`code`, not on the status. Failures may also carry\n`errors: [{\"field\": …, \"detail\": …}]` for field-level highlighting.\n\nQuote the `request_id` when reporting a problem; it links the response to the\nserver log and to any audit record.\n",
    "title": "Restaurant Reservation & Food-Ordering System",
    "version": "0.1.0"
  },
  "openapi": "3.1.0",
  "paths": {
    "/": {
      "get": {
        "description": "Return the service name, version and the API base path.\n\nDeliberately discloses nothing beyond what is already public in the\nrepository.",
        "operationId": "root__get",
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "additionalProperties": true,
                  "title": "Response Root  Get",
                  "type": "object"
                }
              }
            },
            "description": "Successful Response"
          }
        },
        "summary": "Service index",
        "tags": [
          "meta"
        ]
      }
    },
    "/api/v1/auth/me": {
      "get": {
        "description": "Return the caller's id, role and token expiry.\n\nRequires a valid bearer token. Returns 401 with\n`WWW-Authenticate: Bearer` (RFC 6750 §3) when it is absent or invalid,\ncarrying code 30001 or 30002 so the frontend can tell \"never logged in\"\nfrom \"session lapsed\".",
        "operationId": "me_api_v1_auth_me_get",
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "$ref": "#/components/schemas/Envelope_CurrentIdentity_"
                }
              }
            },
            "description": "Successful Response"
          },
          "401": {
            "description": "Missing (30001) or invalid/expired (30002) token"
          }
        },
        "security": [
          {
            "HTTPBearer": []
          }
        ],
        "summary": "Current identity",
        "tags": [
          "auth"
        ]
      }
    },
    "/api/v1/health": {
      "get": {
        "description": "Return process liveness without touching the database.\n\nKept dependency-free on purpose: when MySQL is down we still want the\ncontainer to report that the *process* is alive, so the orchestrator\nrestarts the database rather than killing the API in a loop.\n\nSettings are read from `app.state`, not from `get_settings()`, so an app\nbuilt with custom settings (tests) reports its own environment rather\nthan the process-wide one.\n\nWrapped in the frozen envelope like every other endpoint (manual §4.2),\nso the health payload is under ``data``. The container HEALTHCHECK only\nasserts an HTTP 200, so the wrapper does not disturb it.",
        "operationId": "health_api_v1_health_get",
        "responses": {
          "200": {
            "content": {
              "application/json": {
                "schema": {
                  "$ref": "#/components/schemas/Envelope_HealthResponse_"
                }
              }
            },
            "description": "Successful Response"
          }
        },
        "summary": "Liveness probe",
        "tags": [
          "health"
        ]
      }
    },
